hadiths[65]{hadithnumber,text}:
"617","""I came to the Messenger of Allah while he was sitting in the shade of the Ka'bah."" He said: ""He saw me approaching and he said: 'They are lost on the Day of Judgment! By the Lord of the Ka'bah!'"" He said: ""I said t myself: Woe is me! Perhaps something has been revealed about me!'"" He said: ""So I said: 'Who are they, and may my father and mother be ransomed for you.' So the Messenger of Allah said: 'They are those who have much, except for who says like this, and this, and this and motioned with his hand to his front, and t his right, and to his left.' Then he said: 'By the One in Whose Hand is my soul! No man will die, leaving a camel or a cow that he did not pay Zakat on, except that it will come on the Day of Judgment larger and fatter than it was, they will tread him under their hooves and butt him with their horns, all of them; such that when the last of them has had a turn, the first returns to him, until he is judged before the people.'"""
"618","Abu Hurairah narrated :that the Prophet said: ""When you pay the Zakat you have fulfilled what is required of you"
"619","""We used to hope that an intelligent Bedouin would show up to question the Prophet while we were with him. So one while we were with him, a Bedouin came, kneeling in front of the Prophet, and he said: 'O Muhammad, your messenger came to us and told us that you say that Allah sent you.' So the Prophet say: 'Yes.' He said, 'So, (swear) by the One who raised the heaves, and spread out the earth, and erected the mountains; has Allah sent you?' The Prophet said, 'Yes.' He said: 'Your messenger told us that you say that there are five prayers required from us in a day and a night.' The Prophet said, 'Yes.' He said, 'By the One Who sent you, has Allah ordered that you you?' He said, 'Yes.' He said, 'Your messenger told us that you say that we are required to fast for a month out of the year.' He said, 'He told the truth.' He said, 'By the One Who sent you, has Allah ordered that you?' The Prophet said, 'Yes.' He said, 'Your messenger told us that Zakat is required from our wealth.' The Prophet said, 'He told the truth.' He said, 'By the One Who sent you, has Allah ordered you that?' The Prophet said, 'Yes.' He said, 'Your messenger told us that you say that we are required to perform Hajj to Allah's House if able to undertake the journey.' The Prophet said, 'Yes.' He said, 'By the One Who sent you, has Allah Commanded you that?' (The Prophet said:) 'Yes.' So he said: 'By the One Who sent you with the Truth, I will not leave any of them, nor surpass them.' Then he got up quickly (leaving). The Prophet said: 'If the Bedouin told the truth, then he will enter Paradise.'"""
"620","the Messenger of Allah said: ""I have exempted charity on horses and slaves. So bring charity for silver, one Dirham for every forty Dirham. There is nothing for me (to collect) on one hundred and ninety Dirham, so when it reaches two hundred, then five Dirham of it (are due)."""
"621","Ziyad bin Ayyub Al-Baghdadi, Ibrahim bin 'Abdullah Al-Harawi, and Muhammad bin Kamil Al-Marwazi narrated to us - the meaning being the same - they said: 'Abbad bin Al-'Awwam narrated to us, from Sufyan bin Husain, from Az-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) wrote the document of Zakat but did not send it out to his workers until he passed away, and he kept it tied to his sword. When he passed away, Abu Bakr acted according to it until he passed away, and (then) 'Umar (acted according to it) until he passed away. In it was written:

""For every five camels, one sheep (is due); for ten, two sheep; for fifteen, three sheep; for twenty, four sheep; for twenty-five, one Bint Makhad, up to thirty-five. If it increases (beyond that), then a Bint Labun is due, up to forty-five. If it increases, then a Hiqqah is due, up to sixty. If it increases, then a Jadha'ah is due, up to seventy-five. If it increases, then two Bint Labuns are due, up to ninety. If it increases, then two Hiqqahs are due, up to one hundred and twenty. If it increases beyond one hundred and twenty, then for every fifty, a Hiqqah, and for every forty, a Bint Labun.

As for sheep: For every forty sheep, one sheep is due, up to one hundred and twenty. If it increases, then two sheep, up to two hundred. If it increases, then three sheep, up to three hundred sheep. If it increases beyond three hundred sheep, then for every hundred sheep, one sheep. Then there is nothing due (in addition) until it reaches four hundred.

Animals that are separate should not be combined, and animals that are combined should not be separated, out of fear of (increasing) the Zakat. Whatever is jointly owned by two partners, they settle between themselves equally. Neither an old, worn-out animal nor one with a defect should be taken for Zakat.""

Az-Zuhri said: ""When the Zakat collector comes, he divides the sheep into three parts: a third that are the best, a third that are average, and a third that are the worst, and the collector takes from the average.""

Az-Zuhri did not mention cattle.

[Abu 'Eisa said:] There are other reports on this topic from Abu Bakr As-Siddiq, from Bahz bin Hakim, from his father, from his grandfather, and from Abu Dharr and Anas.

Abu 'Eisa said: The Hadith of Ibn 'Umar is a Hasan Hadith, and it is acted upon according to the majority of the jurists.

Yunus bin Yazid and more than one other narrator reported this Hadith from Az-Zuhri, from Salim, without making it a Marfu' report (i.e., without attributing it directly to the Prophet ﷺ). It was only Sufyan bin Husain who made it Marfu'."
"622","Abdullah bin Mas'ud narrated that :the Prophet said: ""A Tabi or a Tabi'ah (is due) on thirty cows"
"623","""The Prophet sent me to Yemen and ordered me to collect a Tabi or a Tabi'ah on every thirty cows, a Musinnah on every forty, a Dinar for every Halim, or its equivalent of Ma'afir.""Abu Eisa said: This Hadith is Hasan. Some of them reported this Hadith from Sufyan, from Al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Masruq: ""The Prophet sent Mu'adh to Yemen and ordered him to take..."" and this is more authentic"
"624","Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, who said: I asked Abu 'Ubaydah ibn 'Abd Allah, ""Do you recall anything from 'Abd Allah?"" He replied, ""No."""
"625","the Messenger of Allah sent Mu'adh to Yemen and said to him: ""You are going to a people from the People of the Book, so invite them to testify that none has the right to be worshipped but Allah, and that I am the Messenger of Allah. If they comply with that, then inform them that Allah has made five prayers obligatory upon them in a day and a night. If they comply with that, then inform then that Allah has ordained a charity upon their wealth, which is to be taken from the rich among them and given to the poor among them. If they comply with that, then beware of their most precious wealth, and protect yourself from the supplication of the oppressed, for there is no barrier between it and Allah."""
"626","the Prophet said: ""There is no charity due on less than five camels, and there is no charity due on what is less than five Uqiyah (of silver), and there is no charity due on what is less than five Wasaq."""
"627","(Another chain of narration that) Abu Sa'eed Al-Khudri narrated that :the Prophet said (similar t Hadith no)"
"628","Abu Hurairah narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""There is no charity due from a Muslim for his horse nor his slave"
"629","Ibn Umar narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""A Ziqq is due for every ten Ziqq of honey"
"630","Nafi said: ""Umar bin Adbul-Aziz asked me about charity due on honey, so I said to him: 'We do not have honey that we could give charity from, but Al-Mughirah bin Hakim informed us that he (pbuh) said: 'There is no charity due on honey.' So Umar said: 'That is fair enough for me.' So he wrote to the people that is be lifted, meaning (the duty of paying it) from them."""
"631","the Messenger of Allah said: ""Whoever acquired wealth, then there is no Zakat on it until the Hawl has passed (while it is in his possession)."""
"632","# Translation of the Hadith

**Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated to us, Ayyūb narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with both of them), who said:**

""Whoever acquires wealth, there is no zakāh upon it until a full lunar year passes over it in the possession of its owner.""

**Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** ""And this narration is more authentic (*aṣaḥḥ*) than the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam.""

**Abū ʿĪsā said:** ""And Ayyūb, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, and others besides them narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, as a *mawqūf* (a statement attributed to the Companion only) narration.""

""ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam is weak (*ḍaʿīf*) in ḥadīth. Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, and others among the scholars of ḥadīth declared him weak, and he is prone to frequent error (*kathīr al-ghalaṭ*).""

""It has been narrated from more than one of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that there is no zakāh upon acquired wealth until a full lunar year passes over it.""

""Upon this position are Mālik ibn Anas, al-Shāfiʿī, Aḥmad, and Isḥāq (may Allāh have mercy on them all).""

""Some of the scholars of knowledge said: If a person possesses wealth upon which zakāh is obligatory, then zakāh is due upon it (i.e., the acquired wealth is combined with it). However, if he possesses nothing besides the acquired wealth that reaches the niṣāb (minimum threshold), then no zakāh is obligatory upon the acquired wealth until a full lunar year passes over it. If he acquires wealth before the year has elapsed, then he pays zakāh on the acquired wealth together with his other wealth upon which zakāh has become obligatory.""

""Upon this position are Sufyān al-Thawrī and the people of Kūfah (may Allāh have mercy on them all)."""
"633","Ibn Abbas narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""Two Qiblahs in one land are of no benefit"
"634","Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Jarīr narrated to us, from Qābūs, through this chain of transmission, something similar to it. In this chapter, there are narrations from Saʿīd ibn Zayd and the grandfather of Ḥarb ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Thaqafī. Abū ʿĪsā [al-Tirmidhī] said: The ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās has also been narrated from Qābūs ibn Abī Ẓabyān, from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, in a mursal form. The practice according to the generality of the scholars is that when a Christian embraces Islam, the jizyah (poll tax) upon his person is waived. The statement of the Prophet ﷺ, “There are no ʿushūr (taxes) upon the Muslims,” refers specifically to the jizyah upon the person. The ḥadīth itself clarifies this, as he ﷺ said, “Indeed, the ʿushūr are upon the Jews and the Christians, and there are no ʿushūr upon the Muslims.”"
"635","""The Messenger of Allah delivered a sermon to us, and said: 'O you women! Give charity, even if it is from your jewelry, for indeed you will make up most of the people of Hell on the Day of Judgment.'"""
"636","# Scholarly Translation of the Hadith

**Narrated by Mahmūd ibn Ghaylān**, who narrated from Abū Dāwūd, from Shuʿbah, from al-Aʿmash, who said: ""I heard Abū Wāʾil narrating from ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, the nephew of Zaynab, the wife of ʿAbdullāh — from Zaynab, the wife of ʿAbdullāh — from the Prophet ﷺ, a narration similar to the aforementioned.""

Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said: **""This narration is more authentic (aṣaḥḥ) than the ḥadīth of Abū Muʿāwiyah. Abū Muʿāwiyah erred in his narration, for he reported it as: 'From ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, from the nephew of Zaynab.' However, the correct version is: 'From ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, the nephew of Zaynab.'""

He further stated: **""It has also been narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he regarded jewellery (al-ḥulī) as being subject to zakāh.""**

**""However, there is discussion (maqāl) regarding the chain of narration (isnād) of this ḥadīth.""**

**""The scholars have differed on this matter. Some of the scholars from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and the Tābiʿūn held the view that jewellery made of gold and silver is subject to zakāh. This was the position of Sufyān al-Thawrī and ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak.""**

**""However, some of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ — among them Ibn ʿUmar, ʿĀʾishah, Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, and Anas ibn Mālik — held that there is no zakāh on jewellery. This was likewise narrated from some of the jurists among the Tābiʿūn, and it is the position of Mālik ibn Anas, al-Shāfiʿī, Aḥmad, and Isḥāq.""**"
"637","**Narrated by Qutaybah, narrated to us Ibn Luhay'ah, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that two women came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) with gold bracelets in their hands. He asked them: ""Do you pay its zakah?"" They replied: ""No."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then asked them: ""Would you like Allah to adorn you with bracelets of fire?"" They replied: ""No."" He then said: ""Then pay its zakah."" Abu 'Isa [al-Tirmidhi] commented: This hadith has also been narrated by Al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabah from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb in a similar manner. However, Al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabah and Ibn Luhay'ah are both considered weak (du'afā') in hadith transmission, and nothing authentic is established in this regard from the Prophet (ﷺ).**"
"638","Narrated Mu'adh: that he wrote to the Prophet ﷺ asking him about vegetables - meaning green vegetables (Buqul) - and he said: ""There is nothing due on them.""

Abu 'Eisa said: The chain of narration of this Hadith is not authentic (Sahih), and nothing is proven regarding this matter from the Prophet ﷺ. It is only narrated from Musa bin Talhah from the Prophet ﷺ as a Mursal (broken chain) report. The practice among the people of knowledge is upon this, that there is no Zakat due on vegetables.

Abu 'Eisa said: Al-Hasan is Ibn 'Umarah, and he is considered weak (Da'if) among the people of Hadith. Shu'bah and others declared him weak, and 'Abdullah bin Al-Mubarak abandoned him."
"639","the Messenger of Allah said: ""For what is watered by the heavens and steams, the Ushr is due, and for what is watered by irrigation, half of the  Ushr."""
"640","the Messenger of Allah instituted the Ushr of what was watered by the heavens and steams, or through natural channels, and half of the Ushr for what is watered by irrigation."
"641","Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā narrated to us, al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from al-Muthannā ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, addressed the people and said: “Beware! Whoever is the guardian of an orphan who possesses wealth, let him trade with it and not leave it, lest it be consumed by ṣadaqah (i.e., zakāh).”

Abū ʿĪsā said: This ḥadīth has been narrated only from this chain, and there is discussion regarding its isnād, because al-Muthannā ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ is considered weak in ḥadīth. Some have narrated this ḥadīth from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, attributing it to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and he mentioned this ḥadīth.

The scholars have differed on this matter. A number of the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, held that zakāh is due on the wealth of an orphan. Among them were ʿUmar, ʿAlī, ʿĀʾishah, and Ibn ʿUmar. This is also the view of Mālik, al-Shāfiʿī, Aḥmad, and Isḥāq.

Another group of scholars held that there is no zakāh on the wealth of an orphan. This is the view of Sufyān al-Thawrī and ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak.

ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb is the son of Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and Shuʿayb heard from his grandfather ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr.

Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd spoke critically of the ḥadīth of ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb and said: “In our view, it is weak.” Those who deemed him weak did so because he narrated from the scroll of his grandfather ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr. However, the majority of the ḥadīth scholars rely upon the ḥadīth of ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb and consider it acceptable, among them Aḥmad, Isḥāq, and others."
"642","the Messenger of Allah said: ""The injuries caused by the animal are without liability, and mines are without liability, and wells are without liability, and the Khumus is due on Rikaz."""
"643","""Sahl bin Abi Hathmah came to a gathering of ours, and he narrated that the Messenger of Allah would say: ""When you make an assessment, then take it and leave a third, if you do not leave a third, then leave a quarter."""
"644","Attab bin Asid narrated that :the Prophet said about Zakat on grapevines: ""They are to be assessed just as the date-palm is assessed. Then its Zakat is paid in raisins just as the Zakat for the date-palm is paid in dried dates"
"645","Rafi bin Khadij narrated:""I herd the Messenger of Allah saying: 'The one who works in collecting charity in truth is like the one who fights in the cause of Allah until he returns"
"646","Anas bin Malik narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""The one who commits transgression with charity is like the one who does not pay it"
"647","the Prophet said: ""When the charity collector comes to you, then he should not depart from you except while pleased."""
"648","Jarir narrated :similar (narration to no. 647 with a different chain) from the Prophet"
"649","Awn bin Abi Jubaitah narrated from his father:""The charity collector of the Prophet came to us. So he took the charity from our rich to our poor. I was a orphan boy"
"650","the Messenger of Allah said: ""Whoever begs from the people while he has what he needs, he will come on the Day of Judgment and his begging with be scratches or lacerations, or bite marks on his face."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah! 'How much is it that one needs?' He said: 'Fifty Dirham, or their value in gold.'"""
"651","Yahya bin Adam narrated to us, Sufyan narrated this Hadith to us from Hakim bin Jubair. So Abdullah bin Uthman, the companion of Shu'bah said to him (Sufyan): ""If only someone besides Hakim had narrated this (Hadith)."" Sufyan said to him, ""So what is with Hakim; Shu'bah would not narrate from him?"" He said: ""Yes."" So Sufyan said: ""I heard Zubaid narrating this from Muhammad bin Abdur-Rahman bin Yazid."""
"652","Abdullah bin Amr narrated that :the Prophet said: ""Charity is not lawful for the rich nor for the physically fit"
"653","""During the Farewell Hajj, while the Messener of allah was standing at Arafat, a Bedouin came to him begging while pulling on the edge of his Rida. He gave him something and he left. With that, begging was made unlawful, so the Messenger of Allah said: 'Begging is not lawful for the rich nor for the physically fit, except for the one who is severely poor or in perilous debt. And whoever begs the people (merely) to increase his wealth, then on the Day of Judgment (the wealth he begged for) will be lacerations on his face and heated coals from Hell will be provided for him to eat. Whoever wishes, let him take a little, and whoever wishes, then let him take a lot.'"""
"654","Narrator mentioned in hadith:There is another chain with a similar narration"
"655","""During the time of the Messenger of Allah, a man suffered a loss on fruits that he had sold, resulting in more debt. The Messenger of Allah said: 'Give him charity.' So the people gave him charity but it did not cover his debt. So the Messenger of Allah said to his debtors: 'Take what you have and there is nothing for you but that.'"""
"656","Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Makki ibn Ibrahim and Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Duba'i al-Sadusi narrated to us, they both said: Bahz ibn Hakim narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said:

The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, when something was brought to him, he would ask: ""Is it charity (sadaqah) or a gift (hadiyyah)?"" If they said it was charity, he would not eat from it, and if they said it was a gift, he ate from it.

He (the narrator) said: In this chapter (bab), there are narrations from Salman, Abu Hurayrah, Anas, al-Hasan ibn 'Ali, Abu 'Amirah (the grandfather of Ma'ruf ibn Wasil, whose name is Rushayd ibn Malik), Maymun ibn Mihran, Ibn 'Abbas, 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, Abu Rafi', and 'Abdullah ibn 'Alqamah.

This hadith has also been narrated from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Alqamah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi 'Aqil, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him.

The grandfather of Bahz ibn Hakim's name is Mu'awiyah ibn Haydah al-Qushayri.

Abu 'Isa (al-Tirmidhi) said: The hadith of Bahz ibn Hakim is a hasan (good) and gharib (uncommon/singular) hadith."
"657","the Messenger of Allah sent a man from Banu Makhzun to collect charity, so he said to Abu Rafi: ""Accompany me so that perhaps you may have some of it."" So he said: ""Not until I ask the Messenger of Allah."" So he went to the Prophet to ask him, and he said: ""Charity is not lawful for us, and to be the Mawda of a people to be the same as them."""
"658","# Hadith of Salmān ibn ʿĀmir (may Allah be pleased with him)

**Qutaybah narrated to us — Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us — from ʿĀsim al-Aḥwal — from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn — from al-Rabāb — from her paternal uncle, Salmān ibn ʿĀmir, who traced it back to the Prophet ﷺ, who said:**

> ""When one of you breaks his fast, let him break it upon dates, for indeed it is a blessing (barakah). And if he does not find dates, then upon water, for indeed it is purifying (ṭahūr).""

**And he ﷺ also said:**

> ""Charity given to a poor person is a single act of charity (ṣadaqah), and charity given to a kinsman is twofold: charity and the upholding of the ties of kinship (ṣilah).""

**He (the narrator) further stated:** In this chapter there are also narrations from Zaynab, the wife of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, and from Jābir, and from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them all).

**Abū ʿĪsā (al-Tirmidhī) said:** The hadith of Salmān ibn ʿĀmir is a *ḥasan* (good) hadith.

**Al-Rabāb** is Umm al-Rāʾiḥ bint Ṣulayʿ.

**And thus** Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from ʿĀsim, from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, from al-Rabāb, from Salmān ibn ʿĀmir, from the Prophet ﷺ, something similar to this hadith.

**And Shuʿbah** narrated from ʿĀsim, from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, from Salmān ibn ʿĀmir — **but he did not mention** ""from al-Rabāb"" in his narration.

**The hadith of Sufyān al-Thawrī and Ibn ʿUyaynah is more authentic (aṣaḥḥ).**

**And thus** Ibn ʿAwn and Hishām ibn Ḥassān also narrated, from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, from al-Rabāb, from Salmān ibn ʿĀmir.

---

*Transmitted by Imām Abū ʿĪsā Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī (may Allah have mercy upon him) in his Jāmiʿ.*"
"659","It was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maddūyah, who reported from al-Aswad ibn ʿĀmir, from Sharīk, from Abū Ḥamzah, from al-Shaʿbī, from Fāṭimah bint Qays, who said:

""I asked — or the Prophet ﷺ was asked — about zakāh. He ﷺ said: 'Indeed, in wealth there is a right beyond zakāh.' Then he recited this verse from Sūrah al-Baqarah: *'Righteousness is not that you turn your faces toward the east or the west...'* (until the end of the verse)."""
"660","Fatimah bint Qais narrated that :the Prophet said: ""Indeed there is a duty on wealth aside from Zakat"
"661","the Messenger of Allah said: ""None gives charity from Tayyib - and Allah does not accept but Tayyib - but that Ar-Rahman accepts it with His Right (Hand). Even if it is a date, it is nurtured in the Hand of Ar-Rahman until it is greater than a mountain, just as one of you nurtures his foal or young camel."""
"662","Narrated Al-Qasim bin Muhammad: ""I heard Abu Hurairah saying: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed Allah accepts charity, and He takes it with His right hand, and He nurtures it for one of you just as one of you nurtures his foal, until a morsel becomes like the size of (the mountain of) Uhud.""'"" And the confirmation of that is in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Sublime: ""And He is the One who accepts repentance from His servants"" and ""takes the charities"" and ""Allah eliminates usury and nurtures charities.""

[Abu Eisa said:] This Hadith is Hasan Sahih. Something similar to this has been reported from 'Aishah, from the Prophet (ﷺ).

More than one of the people of knowledge have said, concerning this Hadith and what resembles it among the narrations about the Attributes and the descent of the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, every night to the lowest heaven: ""These narrations are established as valid and are to be believed in. They should not be imagined [in a bodily sense], nor should one ask 'how?'"" This has been reported from Malik, Sufyan bin 'Uyainah, and 'Abdullah bin Al-Mubarak, who said concerning these Hadiths: ""Accept them as they are without asking how."" This is likewise the position of the people of knowledge among the people of the Sunnah and the community (Ahl as-Sunnah wal-Jama'ah).

As for the Jahmiyyah, they rejected these narrations and said: ""This is anthropomorphism (Tashbih)."" Allah, Mighty and Sublime, mentioned in more than one place in His Book the Hand, the Hearing, and the Sight, but the Jahmiyyah reinterpreted these verses, explaining them differently from how the people of knowledge explained them. They said: ""Allah did not create Adam with His Hand,"" and they said: ""The meaning of 'Hand' here is 'Power.'""

Ishaq bin Ibrahim said: ""Anthropomorphism only occurs if one says 'a hand like a hand' or 'similar to a hand,' or 'a hearing like a hearing' or 'similar to a hearing.' If one says 'a hearing like a hearing' or 'similar to a hearing,' then this is anthropomorphism. But if one says, as Allah the Exalted said, 'Hand,' 'Hearing,' and 'Sight,' without saying 'how,' and without saying 'similar to a hearing' or 'like a hearing,' then this is not anthropomorphism. This is as Allah the Exalted said in His Book: 'There is nothing like unto Him, and He is the All-Hearing, the All-Seeing.'"""
"663","Anas narrated that :the Prophet was asked which fast was most virtuous after Ramadan? He said: ""Sha'ban in honor of Ramadan"" He said: ""Which charity is best?"" He (pbuh) said: ""Charity in Ramadan"
"664","Anas bin Malik narrated that :the Messenger of Allah said: ""Indeed charity extinguishes the Lord's anger and it protects against the evil death"
"665","Umm Bujaid - and she was one of those who gave the pledge to the Prophet - she said to the Messenger of Allah: ""There is a needy person who stands outside my door, but I cannot find anything to give to him."" So the Messenger of Allah said to her: ""If you do not find anything to give him except a burnt trotter then hand it over to him."""
"666","""The Messenger of Allah gave to me on the Day of Hunain, and he was the most hated creature to me. But he did not stop giving to me until he was the most loved creature to me."""
"667","""I was sitting with the Prophet when a woman came to him and said: 'O Messenger of Allah! I gave a slave girl to my mother in charity and she died.' He said: 'Your reward is already established, and your right to inherit her has returned it (that Sadaqah) to you.' She said: 'O Messenger of Allah! There was a month of fasting due on her, shall I perform the fast for her?' He said: 'Fast on her behalf.' She said: 'O Messenger of Allah! She never performed Hajj, shall I perform Hajj for her?' He said: 'Yes, perform Hajj on her behalf.'"""
"668","Umar gave a horse to be used in the cause of Allah. Then he saw it being sold, so he wanted to buy it, but the Prophet said: ""Do not take back what you have given in charity."""
"669","a man said: ""O Messenger of Allah! My mother died, will it benefit her if I give charity on her behalf?"" He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'I have a Makhraf, so bear witness that I have given it in charity on her behalf.'"""
"670","""During a sermon of his in the year of the Farewell Hajj, I heard the Messenger of Allah say: 'A woman is not to spend anything from her husbands house without her husband's permission.' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah! What about food?' He said: 'That is our most virtuous wealth.'"""
"671","the Prophet said: ""When a woman gives in charity from her husband's house, she will get the reward for it, and for her husband is the same as that, and for the trustee is the same as that. The reward of each them will not be decreased at all by the reward of the other, for him is what he earned, and for her is what she spent."""
"672","the Messenger of Allah said: ""When a woman gives from her husband's home, with a good heart, not to spoil it, then she will get a reward similar to his, for her is the good she intended - and the same is for the trustee."""
"673","""We would give Zakat Al-Fitr - when the Messenger of Allah was among us - as a Sa of food, or a Sa of barely, or a Sa of dried dates, or a Sa of raisins, or a Sa of cheese. So we did not stop paying it (like that) until Mu'awiyah arrived in Al-Madinah and talked (about it). Among the things he addressed the people with, he said: 'I see that two Mudd of the wheat of Ash-Sham are equal to a Sa of dried dates.' So the people followed that."" Abu Sa'eed said: ""I will not stop giving it in the manner that I had been giving it."""
"674","the Prophet sent a caller in the roads of Makkah proclaiming ""Sadaqatul-Fitr is required upon every Muslim, male or female, free or slave, young or old; it is two Mudd of wheat or its equivalent of a Sa of food."""
"675","""The Messenger of Allah made Sadaqatul-Fitr an obligation upon the male and female, the free and the bondsmen, as a Sa of dried dates or a Sa of barley."" He said: ""So the people equated that to half a Sa of wheat."""
"676","Ibn Umar narrated:""The Messenger of Allah made Sadaqatul-Fitr of Ramadan an obligation - a Sa of dried dates or a Sa of barley - required upon every free person and slave"
"677","Ibn Umar narrated that :the Messenger of Allah would order paying the Zakat before going to the Salat on the day of Fitr"
"678","Ali narrated:""Al-Abbas asked the Messenger of Allah about paying his charity (Zakat) in advance before its time is due (by being in possession for a whole year)"
"679","Ali narrated that :the Prophet said to Umar: ""We have taken this year's Zakat from Al-Abbas in the previous year"
"680","the Messenger of Allah saying: ""For one of you to go out early to gather firewood and carry it on his back so that he can give charity from it and be free of need from the people, is better for him than to ask a man who may give that to him or refuse. Indeed the upper hand (giving) is more virtuous than the lower hand (receiving), and begin with (those who are) your dependants."""
"681","the Messenger of Allah said: ""Asking is a labor that toils on a man's face, except if a man asks for something from the Sultan (ruler), or he asks for something that he cannot do without."""
